
Experiment 2: Port Scanning and Information Gathering with Nmap

1. Theory
Port scanning is an active reconnaissance method used to discover live hosts, open/closed ports, running network services, and underlying operating systems:
- TCP SYN Scan (-sS): Also known as a "Half-Open" scan. The scanner sends a SYN packet. If the target responds with SYN/ACK, the port is open (Nmap immediately sends a RST packet to tear down the connection before completion). If it returns RST/ACK, the port is closed.
- TCP Connect Scan (-sT): Completes the full three-way handshake (SYN -> SYN/ACK -> ACK). Used when raw socket privileges are unavailable (such as by non-root users).
- UDP Scan (-sU): Sends raw UDP packets to target ports. If an ICMP Type 3 Code 3 (Port Unreachable) error is returned, the port is closed. If an application responds or no response is received, it is categorized as open|filtered.

2. Installation Commands

Ubuntu / Debian / Kali Linux:
sudo apt update && sudo apt install nmap -y

Fedora / RHEL:
sudo dnf install nmap -y

macOS (Homebrew):
brew install nmap

Windows:
Download and run the binary installer from https://nmap.org/download.html

3. Practical Commands and Execution

A. Host Discovery (Ping Sweep across a subnet):
sudo nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24

B. Stealth TCP SYN Scan on Specific Ports:
sudo nmap -sS -p 21,22,80,443,3306 192.168.1.50

C. UDP Port Scan:
sudo nmap -sU -p 53,67,123,161 192.168.1.50

D. Service Version & OS Fingerprinting:
sudo nmap -sV -O --traceroute 192.168.1.50

E. Comprehensive Aggressive Scan:
sudo nmap -A -T4 192.168.1.50
